/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Entropy crosses a boundary in only two ways: with heat and with flowing mass.
   - S₂ − S₁ is the change in the system’s entropy, in kJ/K; Qⱼ is a heat transfer in kJ (positive into the system) that crosses the boundary where the boundary temperature is Tⱼ in K; Qⱼ/Tⱼ is the entropy that heat carries; Sgen ≥ 0 is the entr
   - For an isolated system no heat and no mass cross, so S₂ − S₁ = Sgen ≥ 0: the principle of increase of entropy is the special case of this balance.
   - A control volume also has streams of mass, and each kilogram brings its specific entropy s with it.
   - dScv/dt = Σ Q̇ⱼ/Tⱼ + Σ ṁᵢsᵢ − Σ ṁₑsₑ + Ṡgen dScv/dt is the rate of change of entropy inside, in kW/K; Q̇ⱼ is a heat rate in kW crossing where the boundary is at Tⱼ; ṁᵢ and ṁₑ are the mass flow rates in kg/s at each inlet and exit; sᵢ and sₑ
   - In steady flow nothing inside changes, so dScv/dt = 0.
   - Each kW/K generated costs T₀Ṡgen kW of work, where T₀ is the temperature of the surroundings in K (the Gouy-Stodola relation).
   - A macrostate is what we measure: p, V, T.
   - k = 1.381 × 10−23 J/K is Boltzmann’s constant, the gas constant per molecule (R̄ divided by Avogadro’s number); W is the number of microstates that give the same macrostate.
   - Let a gas double its volume freely, as in the free-expansion example of reversible and irreversible processes.
*/
