/* book Worked example: Q: Air flows steadily through a compressor at 0.5 kg/s. It enters at 100 kPa and 300 K and leaves at 400 kPa and 480 K. The power input is 95 kW, and the casing loses heat to the surroundings at 300 K. Take cp = 1.005 kJ/(kg·K) and R = 0.287 kJ/(kg·K), and neglect kinetic and potential energy. Find (a) the heat lost, (b) the entropy change of the air per kg, (c) the rate of entropy generation, (d) the lost work, and (e) the power an isentropic compressor would need between the same pressures.
   1. (a) Steady flow energy equation: the air gains ṁcp(T₂ − T₁) = 0.5 × 1.005 × (480 − 300) = 90.45 kW. The power in is 95 kW, so heat lost Q̇out = 95 − 90.45 = 4.55 kW.
   2. (b) Δs = cp ln(T₂/T₁) − R ln(p₂/p₁) = 1.005 ln(480/300) − 0.287 ln(400/100) = 1.005 × 0.4700 − 0.287 × 1.3863 = 0.4724 − 0.3979 = 0.0745 kJ/(kg·K).
   3. Entropy carried out by the air: ṁΔs = 0.5 × 0.0745 = 0.0372 kW/K.
   4. Heat term: the heat leaves where the boundary is at T₀ = 300 K, so Σ Q̇/T = −4.55/300 = −0.0152 kW/K.
   5. (c) Ṡgen = ṁ(s₂ − s₁) − Σ Q̇/T = 0.0372 − (−0.0152) = 0.0524 kW/K. It is positive, so the process is possible and irreversible.
   6. (d) Lost work = T₀Ṡgen = 300 × 0.0524 = 15.7 kW.
   7. (e) Isentropic exit temperature: T₂ₛ = T₁(p₂/p₁)R/cp = 300 × 40.2856 = 300 × 1.4857 = 445.7 K. Isentropic power = 0.5 × 1.005 × (445.7 − 300) = 73.2 kW, so the isentropic efficiency is 73.2/95 = 0.77.
   Answer: Heat lost 4.55 kW; Δs = +0.0745 kJ/(kg·K); Ṡgen = 0.0524 kW/K; lost work 15.7 kW; an isentropic compressor would need 73.2 kW, an isentropic efficiency of 0.77. */
