/* book exam traps: Work carries no entropy. Adding a Ẇ/T term to the balance is a common slip; there is no such term. | Each heat term uses the boundary temperature where the heat crosses, with its sign: heat lost gives −Q̇/T₀. Dropping it in the worked example gives Ṡgen = 0.0372 instead of 0.0524 kW/K. | s₂ > s₁ alone does not prove irreversibility when heat crosses the boundary, and s₂ < s₁ is allowed when enough heat leaves. Only Ṡgen ≥ 0 is the test. | Lost work T₀Ṡgen is not the same as actual minus isentropic power: 15.7 kW against 95 − 73.2 = 21.8 kW in the example, because the hotter exit air still carries some useful energy. | In S = k ln W, k is per molecule; for a bulk gas Nk = mR. */
