/* book exam traps: S = 0 at 0 K is for a perfect crystal at equilibrium. Glasses, solid solutions and crystals with frozen-in defects keep residual entropy, so do not apply zero blindly. | Absolute zero is unattainable, not just difficult. A finite number of adiabatic demagnetisation steps never reaches exactly 0 K; microkelvin is not zero. | Do not integrate S = cp ln T down to 0 K. Use cp = aT3 and the integral aT3/3 over the low-temperature range. | cp and cv tend to zero as T → 0 K. Treating them as constant near absolute zero gives a divergent, negative entropy. | Entropy change between two states needs no third law. Absolute entropy, as in tables, does need the third-law zero. */
